WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.528
This is Wallet Vitals, an evidence grounded risk copilot for Aave V3 on
Ethereum.

2
00:00:05.528 --> 00:00:10.502
This walkthrough uses the real application deployed on onebattle dot
win.

3
00:00:10.502 --> 00:00:13.128
Paste a public wallet address to begin.

4
00:00:13.128 --> 00:00:17.550
There is no login, wallet connection, signature, or transaction.

5
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.508
Here is the result for a public example address.

6
00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:29.290
The reconstructed health factor is one point one four zero seven, and
the liquidation buffer is twelve point three three percent.

7
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:36.221
The green verification line confirms that the position matches Aave's
own account data at the same evidence block.

8
00:00:37.321 --> 00:00:42.873
The stress ladder tests five, ten, and twenty percent declines in
collateral prices.

9
00:00:42.873 --> 00:00:45.950
The first two scenarios are in the danger band.

10
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.766
Only the twenty percent scenario crosses the tested liquidation
boundary.

11
00:00:50.766 --> 00:00:57.055
These are sensitivity tests with fixed debt value, not price forecasts
or a promise of safety.

12
00:00:58.155 --> 00:01:02.175
A second check compares two genuinely different indexed blocks.

13
00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:10.216
In this example, collateral and debt are unchanged at the displayed
precision, and the reported health factor change is zero.

14
00:01:10.216 --> 00:01:12.485
The application shows that honestly.

15
00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:16.830
On a first check, it explicitly says that no baseline is available.

16
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:21.088
Open the evidence receipt to inspect the source.

17
00:01:21.088 --> 00:01:26.129
The Graph supplies indexed positions and the basis for snapshot
comparisons.

18
00:01:26.129 --> 00:01:33.320
The receipt includes its deployment identifier, the exact Ethereum
block, query time, and calculation rules.

19
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:40.108
These details make the result inspectable instead of asking a judge to
trust a generated explanation.

20
00:01:41.208 --> 00:01:46.479
Prices come from Aave's oracle, read at the same block hash as the
position evidence.

21
00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:51.437
The application reconstructs the account and compares it with the pool
contract.

22
00:01:51.437 --> 00:01:53.006
A mismatch stops analysis.

23
00:01:53.006 --> 00:02:01.416
The receipt also states the assumptions, and makes clear that reading an
oracle does not establish each underlying feed's update time.

24
00:02:02.516 --> 00:02:07.490
The optional AI layer explains supplied evidence rather than calculating
risk.

25
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.528
The label tells you whether an explanation came from AI or deterministic
logic.

26
00:02:12.528 --> 00:02:20.990
Asking what breaks first produces a deterministic answer, so a danger
label cannot be confused with the actual liquidation boundary.

27
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.026
The application never signs or executes trades.

28
00:02:25.126 --> 00:02:28.954
Here is a real failure case from another public address.

29
00:02:28.954 --> 00:02:35.844
Its indexed data contains a negative scaled balance, so the application
refuses to produce a report.

30
00:02:35.844 --> 00:02:42.178
Stale blocks, indexing errors, unsupported e Mode, and account
mismatches are also rejected.

31
00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:47.886
The Graph integration is part of the evidence pipeline, not a decorative
API call.

32
00:02:48.986 --> 00:02:51.545
Wallet Vitals is live and open source.

33
00:02:51.545 --> 00:02:57.287
Public reports have a twenty four hour lifetime, and the demo has
bounded API usage.

34
00:02:57.287 --> 00:03:02.476
Open the application, run your own read only check, and inspect the
receipt.

35
00:03:02.476 --> 00:03:07.457
Wallet Vitals turns an opaque risk score into a diagnosis with evidence.
